WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Performance evaluation operates at two connected levels.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:09.080
First, the organization asks whether the AIMS is operating as intended.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:17.340
Second, it asks whether relevant AI systems, processes, and controls are producing acceptable results.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:26.320
A dashboard full of model metrics is not enough if scope is outdated, owners are unclear, assessments are missing, or management never acts on evidence.

5
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:32.260
Conversely, a tidy audit file is weak if deployed systems are drifting without response.

6
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.420
Monitoring and measurement should be designed deliberately.

7
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:46.240
For each measure, define what is measured, why it matters, method, data source, frequency, owner, threshold, escalation, reporting, and retention.

8
00:00:46.340 --> 00:01:08.820
Useful measures may include inventory completeness, assessment completion, control implementation, monitoring coverage, incident trends, supplier-review completion, corrective-action aging, policy exceptions, competence gaps, unapproved AI use, system-performance indicators, user complaints, override rates, and appeal trends.

9
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.880
Measures should create information that someone can use.

10
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.520
The organization should avoid vanity metrics that are easy to count but weakly connected to effectiveness.

11
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:23.940
Counting how many people completed awareness training does not prove they can recognize an unapproved AI use case.

12
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.280
Counting risk assessments does not prove risk treatment was implemented.

13
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:35.900
Good measurement helps owners decide whether controls are suitable, operating, producing evidence, and still aligned with context.

14
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.460
Internal audit evaluates whether the AIMS conforms to applicable requirements and operates effectively.

15
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:57.480
It should be planned, risk informed, objective, evidence based, performed by competent personnel, sufficiently independent from the work being audited, documented, and followed through to corrective action where necessary.

16
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:06.620
Evidence may include interviews, records, samples, observations, monitoring data, decision records, supplier evidence, and test results.

17
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:13.520
A risk-informed audit program spends more attention where consequences, change, or uncertainty are greater.

18
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:23.400
Internal audit is not the same as technical model evaluation, penetration testing, legal review, or certification audit, though each can provide supporting evidence.

19
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.140
Management review is also distinct.

20
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:41.360
It is a top-management process that considers changes in context, interested-party expectations, AIMS performance, audit results, risks, impacts, incidents, objectives, resources, and opportunities for improvement.

21
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:49.300
The output should be decisions about improvement, AIMS changes, resources, priorities, objectives, risk acceptance, and escalation.

22
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:55.800
Review should produce direction, not just minutes, and the next review should check whether prior actions happened.

23
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:06.220
This closes the performance-evaluation loop: monitoring produces signals, audit tests evidence, management review sets direction, and assigned owners turn decisions into action.

24
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.840
Without that chain, measurement becomes reporting theater.

25
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.860
With it, performance evaluation becomes a source of managed improvement.

26
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.800
and better prioritization.
